WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
What a working engineer does with this.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:15.000
Before the first run: render a few examples with the real chat template and print the exact span that will be scored, check that the end-of-turn token is inside it, and check that no example is truncated to nothing.

3
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.467
During the run: log train and validation loss on a fixed schedule, and save checkpoints you might want to pick.

4
00:00:21.747 --> 00:00:32.179
After the run: score the base and tuned models on the same frozen test set with your task metric, read the failures, and score a small general-ability regression set.

5
00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:45.301
Record the configuration (model id and revision, data version, learning rate, epochs, effective batch size, masking, seed) next to the results, so a colleague can reproduce or challenge them.

6
00:00:45.581 --> 00:00:59.000
On model selection: the same discipline lets you compare a fine-tuned small model with a prompted frontier model fairly, because both are scored by the same metric on the same frozen cases, never by their losses.

7
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.656
Lesson 8 adds the one decision this lesson took for granted: which parameters to train.
